# Local residues at places: pole filtration, canonical data, sections

Definition: `AlgebraicCurve_CanonicalLocalResidueInstanceV2`
(`df2a3fdb-1fe8-5809-bd7a-e60e44fefcde`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

For a field extension $F/K$ and a place $v$ of $F/K$ (a valuation subring with principal maximal
ideal containing $K$), the module builds residue maps and the structures they require. A
distinguished irreducible element `uniformizerSubring` of $\mathcal O_v$ lifting `v.uniformizer` is
fixed, and the pole filtration is defined: `poleSubmodule n` is the $K$-submodule $\{f\in F : \pi^n
f\in\mathcal O_v\}$, equal to $\mathcal O_v$ for $n=0$, to `simplePoleSubmodule` for $n=1$,
characterised by $-n\le\operatorname{ord}_v f$ for $f\neq0$, monotone in $n$ with supremum $F$;
`laurentTailCoeff n` is the $K$-linear map sending $f$ to the residue class of $\pi^n f$ in
$\kappa(v)$. On the simple-pole submodule the residue $f\mapsto \overline{\pi f}$ is defined
outright; `localResidueExtend` is a chosen $K$-linear extension of it to $F$, which yields a
`LocalResidueData` and hence the unconditional instance `HasLocalResidue K F`. Subtracting
`higherPoleCorrection` — a linear map vanishing on `poleSubmodule 1` and matching the extension on
the classes of $\pi^{-(m+2)}$, which are linearly independent modulo `poleSubmodule 1` — produces a
`CanonicalLocalResidueDataK`, i.e. a residue vanishing on $\mathcal O_v$, equal to $\overline{\pi
f}$ on simple poles and zero on $\pi^{-(n+1)}$ for $n\ge 1$; this gives the instance
`HasCanonicalLocalResidueK K F`. Two such data agree on all pole submodules when $K\to\kappa(v)$ is
surjective. For larger residue fields, `CoefficientFieldSection` is a $K$-algebra map
$\kappa(v)\to\mathcal O_v$ splitting the residue map, `CanonicalLocalResidueDataS \sigma` adds
vanishing on $\sigma(a)\pi^{-(n+1)}$, $n\ge 1$, and such data again agree on pole submodules. A
parallel development replaces $\mathcal O_v$ by its adic completion $\widehat{\mathcal O}_v$: the
level-one evaluation `lg37_residueHat` onto $\kappa(v)$ is surjective with kernel the maximal ideal,
the completion is Henselian and local, separable monic polynomials lift roots from $\kappa(v)$
uniquely, `Lg37CompletionSection` records a $K$-algebra section of `lg37_residueHat`, one such
section is obtained from a primitive element of $\kappa(v)/K$ together with a lifted root of its
minimal polynomial, and every element has a unique truncated Taylor expansion
$\sum_{i<n}\sigma(a_i)\pi^i$ modulo $\mathfrak m^n$. Auxiliary instances provide the residue-field
algebra and tower structure for the restriction of a place along $F\subseteq F'$, and the
nonvanishing of $\operatorname{Tr}_{\kappa(v)/K}$ (`HasSeparableResidue`) when $K$ is perfect and
all residue fields are finite over $K$.

**Relation to Mathlib.** The residue data structures, the pole filtration, the coefficient-field and
completion sections are the project's own notions; they are built on Mathlib's `AdicCompletion`,
`HenselianLocalRing`/`HenselianRing`, `IsLocalRing.ResidueField` and discrete valuation ring
machinery. Mathlib has no Cohen coefficient-field theorem, which is why sections are introduced as
data here.

**Where it is used.** These constructions discharge the `HasLocalResidue`,
`HasCanonicalLocalResidueK` and `HasSeparableResidue` hypotheses carried by the project's
residue-theorem and Riemann–Roch development for function fields of curves, which in turn underlies
the treatment of modular curves in the modularity part of the argument.

